  "solution": {
    "approach": "For each test case we need the product of two real numbers and to print it with two decimal places using half‑up rounding. The straightforward way is to read the numbers, multiply them and format the result. To avoid binary floating‑point rounding issues we use java.math.BigDecimal which can represent the input exactly and provides a setScale method with RoundingMode.HALF_UP. This guarantees the output matches the required rounding rule. The algorithm runs in O(T) time and O(1) extra space besides the input strings.",
    "timeComplexity": "O(T) – one constant‑time multiplication per test case.",
    "spaceComplexity": "O(1) auxiliary space (ignoring input storage).",
    "code": {
      "C": "",
      "C++": "",
      "Java": "import java.io.*;import java.util.*;import java.math.*;public class Main{public static void main(String[] args) throws Exception{BufferedReader br=new BufferedReader(new InputStreamReader(System.in));String line=br.readLine();if(line==null||line.isEmpty())return;int T=Integer.parseInt(line.trim());StringBuilder sb=new StringBuilder();for(int i=0;i<T;i++){StringTokenizer st=new StringTokenizer(br.readLine());BigDecimal a=new BigDecimal(st.nextToken());BigDecimal b=new BigDecimal(st.nextToken());BigDecimal prod=a.multiply(b).setScale(2,RoundingMode.HALF_UP);sb.append(\"Product: \").append(prod.toPlainString());if(i<T-1)sb.append('\\n');}System.out.print(sb.toString());}}",
      "Python3": "",
      "JavaScript": ""
    },
